From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath11k: reset again when the firmware crashes during a
 restart

When ath11k_core_reset() finds a reset in progress, it waits for that one
to complete and skips itself if it does. A restart that the firmware dies
in does complete, though: mac80211 ignores most errors while it reconfigures
the device, and ath11k_mac_op_reconfig_complete() ends the reset. Measured
on a WCN6855 with the second crash 2.6 s into the restart:

  ath11k_pci 0006:01:00.0: firmware crashed: MHI_CB_EE_RDDM
  ath11k_pci 0006:01:00.0: already resetting count 2
  wlan0: failed to set key (0, xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx) to hardware (-108)
  wlan0: failed to set key (1, ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff) to hardware (-108)
  ath11k_pci 0006:01:00.0: failed to transmit frame -108
  ...

The second reset returned, ATH11K_FLAG_CRASH_FLUSH stayed set, and every
frame and WMI command failed with -ESHUTDOWN, with the radio in
ATH11K_STATE_ON, until a system suspend happened to power the device down
and up 15 s later. Nothing in the driver would have restarted it otherwise:
the firmware doesn't report anything any more, and ath11k_mac_op_start()
can't load it.

The skip isn't needed for anything else. The second report of one crash,
from the MHI state change and the control event ring, stops at the
consecutive RDDM check in ath11k_mhi_op_status_cb(), so a reset that finds
another one in progress is for a new crash. Keep waiting for the restart to
end, because ath11k_core_post_reconfigure_recovery() takes a radio that is
still in it for wedged, and then take over from it.

Now that the waiter goes on after complete(), do everything else that ends
the reset before that call: a late write to is_reset would undo the new
reset's, and the wakeup source calls would cut its hold down to
ATH11K_RECOVERY_SETTLE_MS. Set up the completion before raising the count,
so that an earlier reset ending between the two can't be missed. Flush the
restart work too: after a timeout it can still be talking to the device
that is about to be powered down.

Where the skip used to do nothing, a reset now powers the device down and
up, and a suspend can start during the wait. Leave the crash pending for
PM_POST_SUSPEND then, as for any crash during a suspend.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.c | 36 ++++++++++++++++----------
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/mac.c  |  4 +--
 2 files changed, 24 insertions(+), 16 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.c
index 6d67631f5..a0e4ddf11 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.c
@@ -2591,7 +2591,6 @@ static void ath11k_core_reset(struct work_struct *work)
 {
 	struct ath11k_base *ab = container_of(work, struct ath11k_base, reset_work);
 	int reset_count, fail_cont_count;
-	long time_left;
 
 	if (!(test_bit(ATH11K_FLAG_REGISTERED, &ab->dev_flags))) {
 		ath11k_warn(ab, "ignore reset dev flags 0x%lx\n", ab->dev_flags);
@@ -2612,28 +2611,37 @@ static void ath11k_core_reset(struct work_struct *work)
 	    time_before(jiffies, ab->reset_fail_timeout))
 		return;
 
+	reinit_completion(&ab->reset_complete);
 	reset_count = atomic_inc_return(&ab->reset_count);
 
 	if (reset_count > 1) {
-		/* Sometimes it happened another reset worker before the previous one
-		 * completed, then the second reset worker will destroy the previous one,
-		 * thus below is to avoid that.
+		/*
+		 * The firmware crashed again while the previous reset was
+		 * restarting it. Let that restart end first, it can't be reset
+		 * under mac80211. Don't go by how it ended: mac80211 ignores most
+		 * errors, so it ends the same way on firmware that is gone.
 		 */
 		ath11k_warn(ab, "already resetting count %d\n", reset_count);
 
-		reinit_completion(&ab->reset_complete);
-		time_left = wait_for_completion_timeout(&ab->reset_complete,
-							ATH11K_RESET_TIMEOUT_HZ);
+		if (!wait_for_completion_timeout(&ab->reset_complete,
+						 ATH11K_RESET_TIMEOUT_HZ)) {
+			ab->reset_fail_timeout = jiffies + ATH11K_RESET_FAIL_TIMEOUT_HZ;
+			/* Record the continuous recovery fail count when recovery failed*/
+			atomic_inc(&ab->fail_cont_count);
+		}
 
-		if (time_left) {
-			ath11k_dbg(ab, ATH11K_DBG_BOOT, "to skip reset\n");
+		flush_work(&ab->restart_work);
+
+		/* A suspend got in during the wait: stay out of its way. */
+		if (test_bit(ATH11K_FLAG_SYSTEM_SUSPEND, &ab->dev_flags)) {
 			atomic_dec(&ab->reset_count);
+			ath11k_core_schedule_reset(ab);
 			return;
 		}
 
-		ab->reset_fail_timeout = jiffies + ATH11K_RESET_FAIL_TIMEOUT_HZ;
-		/* Record the continuous recovery fail count when recovery failed*/
-		atomic_inc(&ab->fail_cont_count);
+		/* This reset takes over from the previous one. */
+		atomic_set(&ab->reset_count, 1);
+		pm_wakeup_ws_event(ab->recovery_ws, ATH11K_RECOVERY_WAKEUP_MS, false);
 	}
 
 	ath11k_dbg(ab, ATH11K_DBG_BOOT, "reset starting\n");
@@ -2651,8 +2659,8 @@ static void ath11k_core_reset(struct work_struct *work)
 
 	ath11k_dbg(ab, ATH11K_DBG_BOOT, "waiting recovery start...\n");
 
-	time_left = wait_for_completion_timeout(&ab->recovery_start,
-						ATH11K_RECOVER_START_TIMEOUT_HZ);
+	wait_for_completion_timeout(&ab->recovery_start,
+				    ATH11K_RECOVER_START_TIMEOUT_HZ);
 
 	ath11k_hif_irq_disable(ab);
 	ath11k_hif_ce_irq_disable(ab);
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/mac.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/mac.c
index 27663ffdc..83379b5a4 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/mac.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/mac.c
@@ -9306,8 +9306,6 @@ ath11k_mac_op_reconfig_complete(struct ieee80211_hw *hw,
 			 * the recovery has to be done for each radio
 			 */
 			if (recovery_count == ab->num_radios) {
-				atomic_dec(&ab->reset_count);
-				complete(&ab->reset_complete);
 				ab->is_reset = false;
 				atomic_set(&ab->fail_cont_count, 0);
 				/*
@@ -9318,6 +9316,8 @@ ath11k_mac_op_reconfig_complete(struct ieee80211_hw *hw,
 				__pm_relax(ab->recovery_ws);
 				pm_wakeup_ws_event(ab->recovery_ws,
 						   ATH11K_RECOVERY_SETTLE_MS, false);
+				atomic_dec(&ab->reset_count);
+				complete(&ab->reset_complete);
 				ath11k_dbg(ab, ATH11K_DBG_BOOT, "reset success\n");
 			}
 		}
